      "string" : "Information Aggregation in Sequential\nMarkets: A Fixture\nAnonymous\nAbstract\nWe study how sequential traders aggregate private signals. Our\nidentification strategy exploits the staggered rollout of a disclosure\nrule. The effect is statistically significant but economically small.\n1 Introduction\nHerding has been studied extensively since Banerjee (1992) and Bikhchan-\ndani et al. (1992). Later work extends the framework to markets (Avery and\nZemsky, 1998; Smith and Sørensen, 2000). As shown by Acemoglu et al.\n(2011), network structure determines whether learning is efficient; see also\n(Smith and Sørensen, 2000, p. 12) and Kogan (2020); Lee (2021). The coef-\nficient in Table 2 is 0.91 with a confidence interval that crosses one, which is\ndifficult to reconcile with the claim of a robust effect. We define the office-\nspecific identification threshold and afterwards verify the differentiability of\n1"
